622. Design Circular Queue
Problem

Design a Circular Queue with capacity k.

Implement the following methods:

MyCircularQueue(k) → Initializes the queue.
enQueue(value) → Insert an element into the circular queue.
deQueue() → Delete an element from the queue.
Front() → Get the front element.
Rear() → Get the last element.
isEmpty() → Check whether the queue is empty.
isFull() → Check whether the queue is full.
Example
Input

["MyCircularQueue","enQueue","enQueue","enQueue","enQueue",
"Rear","isFull","deQueue","enQueue","Rear"]

[[3],[1],[2],[3],[4],[],[],[],[4],[]]

Output

[null,true,true,true,false,3,true,true,true,4]
Key Observation

A normal queue wastes space after deletions.

Example:

Size = 5

0 1 2 3 4

After inserting

10 20 30
10 20 30 _ _

Remove two elements

_ _ 30 _ _

Even though there is free space,

normal array implementation cannot insert again if rear reaches the end.

Circular Queue solves this by wrapping around.

Possible approaches:

Brute Force (Shift Elements)
Better (Linear Queue using Array)
Circular Queue (Most Optimal)

Approach 1: Brute Force (Shift Elements)
Intuition

Use an array.

Whenever an element is deleted,

shift all remaining elements one position to the left.

Java
class MyCircularQueue {

    int[] arr;
    int size;

    public MyCircularQueue(int k) {

        arr = new int[k];
        size = 0;
    }

    public boolean enQueue(int value) {

        if (size == arr.length)
            return false;

        arr[size++] = value;

        return true;
    }

    public boolean deQueue() {

        if (size == 0)
            return false;

        for (int i = 1; i < size; i++) {

            arr[i - 1] = arr[i];
        }

        size--;

        return true;
    }

    public int Front() {

        return size == 0 ? -1 : arr[0];
    }

    public int Rear() {

        return size == 0 ? -1 : arr[size - 1];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {

        return size == 0;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {

        return size == arr.length;
    }
}

